taiwan's highest civilian honor awarded  to its high-profile guest of honor  top u.s politician nancy pelosi proudly  displayed her new medal on this brief  but brazen visit  she's here in defiance of chinese  warnings and even the advice of her own  president  today the world faces a choice between  democracy  and autocracy  america's determination to preserve  democracy here in taiwan and around the  world  remains ironclad  the gratitude all too clear from the  leader of this self-governed island  which china views as a breakaway region  that it wants to control by force if  necessary  thank you for taking concrete actions to  show your staunch support for taiwan at  this critical moment and for expressing  the us's consistent policy supporting  taiwan's self-defense  as the third most powerful politician in  washington visited various sites she'd  have been all too aware the issue of  taiwan is the most sensitive in u.s  china relations  washington agrees to have formal  diplomatic ties only with beijing and to  keep its contact with taipei more  unofficial under a long-standing policy  of one china  it's why the site of the touring u.s  house speaker has sparked such fury  the us is infringing on china's  sovereignty  in response to pelosi's visit the  chinese military is conducting its  largest drills aimed at taiwan in more  than 25 years they're expected to take  place within these six boxed off areas  encircling the island the main live  firing exercises from the air and the  sea are due to last from thursday  through to sunday now neither side say  they want a war but an accidental  exchange could ignite just that  the g7 group of nations have voiced  concern and say china's moves risk  increasing tensions and destabilizing  the region  taiwan's defense ministry posted this  video to make clear it's ready to fight  if necessary any move either side makes  is now viewed through the prism of the  very real war in ukraine with china and  taiwan both drawing lessons from  russia's invasion  pelosi clearly believes her flying visit  will bolster taiwan  rather than backfire  debra haynes sky news
 

